  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Increasing risk for severe thunderstorms amidst \nincreasingly warm and humid conditions ahead of an approaching \ncold front.\n\nToday marks a shift in the weather pattern from recent days as\nhigh pressure pushes offshore. This will turn winds out of the\nsouth, yielding a much more humid tropical air mass. The process\nof moisture transfer will just be starting today, so dew points\nonly jump back into the mid 60s, but it will set the stage for\nthe pre-frontal environment on Thursday. Highs today reach the\nmid to upper 80s for most, and the 70s in the higher elevations. \nIt looks like the atmosphere should remain capped most of the \nday, although a pop up shower along the terrain could occur in \nthe afternoon. \n\nMoisture return increases overnight along a northward-lifting\nwarm front, which could result in some scattered showers and \nstorms overnight into Thursday morning. How much activity occurs\novernight could have implications on the rest of the day, but\nprobabilities remain rather low overall. At any rate, there will\nlikely be some cloud cover at the very least. Whether or not\nthis clears out later in the morning will have large\nimplications on the overall severe threat. \n\nMore notable forcing arrives Thursday as an upper level trough \nand surface cold front approach from the northwest. However, \nthese forcing features may be somewhat displaced from the local \narea during peak diurnal heating. The front will lag to our\nnorthwest, while the strongest forcing with the trough passes \nto the north. A secondary shortwave may approach but not until \nthe evening. Thus there remains some uncertainty in the coverage\nand timing of any showers and thunderstorms. There could even be\ntwo rounds, dependent upon whether that morning cloud cover\nclears out early enough. If everything does come together \nthough, moderate instability combined with deep-layer shear of\naround 25-35 kt could result in some severe thunderstorms. Mid-\nlevel lapse rate don't look all that unimpressive, so in\naddition to the primary damaging wind threat, large hail is also\ngoing to be a possibility. Wouldn't even be shocked to see a\nsupercell or two. Some of the more aggressive model guidance, \nparticularly the HRRR, does paint some rather impressive veering\nlow-level wind profiles over the region in forecast soundings. \nIf this scenario were to come to fruition, there would also be \na non-zero tornado threat. Not that this is the most likely \nscenario, but worth mentioning at this point. It is also worth \nnoting that the Storm Prediction Center did drop the Slight Risk\narea southward into our area, particularly along the MD/PA \nborder east of the Blue Ridge. This matches the thinking here in\nan increased severe weather threat compared to our thinking the\npast few days.\n\nThe cold front may bisect the area by Friday morning while the\nmid/upper level trough axis remains to the west. Some showers\nand thunderstorms will continue to be possible near and south of\nthe front, although the risk for strong thunderstorms is lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions heading into the weekend, with \nincreasingly hot & humid conditions at the start of next week.\n\nUpper-level zonal flow returns over the Mid-Atlantic by Saturday in \nthe wake of the cold front. A few diurnal showers & thunderstorms \nremain possible throughout the early weekend, but overall conditions \nbecome progressively drier as surface high pressure tracks across \nthe Blue Ridge. Temperatures remain seasonable during this period, \nwith daily highs ranging in the mid-to-upper 80s during the day.\n\nTowards the start of next week, broad upper-level ridging starts to \nbuild over the Southeast. Combined with relatively moist air aloft, \nheat & humidity here will start to feel like peak summertime \nconditions once again, with dewpoints ranging in the low-70s over \nthe metros and highs in the lower 90s by next Monday. 850 mb temp \nanomalies show this pattern only continuing to build in the long-\nterm, which may lead to a warmer-than-average start to September \nshould this pattern persist. Thunderstorm chances could gradually \nincrease during this period as ridging transitions offshore. \nRegardless, anticipate a return to summertime heat & humidity here \nin the Mid-Atlantic should this upper-level setup persist.",
